From 100d76016462d60d1c6da1e1bda84ea72dcbfe95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hibreak <hibreak@localhost>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:12:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sf: optional e-ink engine that drives the panel from the
 client target

With debug.sf.eink_engine=1, the client target queued to the display is
also converted to 8-bit grey in a persistent ION buffer (inside its
surface damage) and handed to the XRZ e-ink kernel driver with
DRM_IOCTL_EINK_UPDATE. Only the 32x32 tiles whose grey value really
changed are updated: apps often report their whole window as damaged,
and the IME window is full height.

The engine converts frames on its own thread. Converting a full client
target takes ~50 ms; done in advanceFrame on SurfaceFlinger's main
thread it held composition to about ten frames a second (atrace:
finishFrame 50-79 ms of each composite). FramebufferSurface now only
waits before releasing the one buffer that is being read.
The conversion loop works on tile-wide chunks without a table lookup
(vectorised) and compares each chunk with the shadow at once; up to
four threads share the rows (debug.sf.eink.convert_threads).

Policy 3 (the default) is policy 2 with the update shapes and ghost
handling of e-readers (Kindle, Kobo, KOReader and Onyx refresh a
widget's or a page's rectangle, never a grid of cells):
- The tile diff only says what changed. Changed tiles closer than
  96 px form one region (a button, a row, the status bar), at most two
  per change, and each region is one update in one mode: grey when a
  quarter of its changed tiles have greys, otherwise the fast mode.
  Policy 2 sent one button as A2 and GL16 tiles that arrived at
  different times, and the "Clear" app mode flashed every tile with
  GC16 on its own.
- A ghost budget counts what was drawn without a flash: fast modes per
  panel, GL16 at half, a gesture once at twice its area. Once 4 panels
  have built up (8 with the kernel's auto-clean on), the next new
  screen is one GC16 of the whole panel, or the next settle a GC16
  over its gesture: the "full refresh every N pages" of e-readers.
  GL16 screen changes left visible ghosts of the previous screens with
  and without auto-clean (camera).
- Every large GC16 is followed by the invisible flush, so the "Clear"
  app mode scrolls in A2 instead of DU.

Policy 2 sorts changes into three classes, built on what
the kernel driver does (measured, see the device tree's
docs/eink-interface.md):
- Tap: black and white tiles go out in A2 (~145 ms), tiles with greys
  in GL16, as at most four rectangles. A change over half the panel (a
  new screen) is collected for up to 120 ms of quiet (360 ms at most),
  since apps draw a new screen in two or three frames, and goes out as
  one GL16 update; a third change in that time makes it motion. No cleanup pass follows: the kernel
  cleans A2 ghosting itself (eink_debug/clean_a2).
- Motion: changes arriving less than 100 ms apart. One update per panel
  slot (90 ms) over one rectangle that only grows during the gesture,
  so the kernel replaces a queued update with the newer one instead of
  replaying a backlog; frames in between are not converted at all.
  Motion ends 250 ms after the last frame, or after the last frame
  that changed pixels.
- Settle: one GL16 over the gesture rectangle 150 ms after the motion.
GC16 is never used automatically, because A2 falls back to a slow
dithered waveform while a GC16 of the area is among the kernel's last
ten updates; after a forced GC16 the engine sends ten invisible updates
to push it out. Policy 1 keeps the previous behaviour (DU motion,
per-tile GL16/GC16 cleanup 800 ms after a change).

Every tunable is a debug.sf.eink.* property, re-read whenever a
property changes, so tuning needs no restart. Per-app and forced
refresh modes come from the vendor.xrz.* properties the vendor composer
used.

The vendor composer on the Bigme HiBreak Pro refreshes the whole panel
for every frame, so small changes such as a key press wait for a
full-panel waveform. The vendor composer commits the first frames after
boot, because the kernel driver sets up its pipeline on those (an
earlier engine update spins in the kernel); then the engine sets
vendor.xrz.disable_commit and takes over.

The client target buffers get GRALLOC_USAGE_SW_READ_OFTEN while the
engine is on, which also keeps them linear.

